<header><p class="enc-kicker">Person</p><h1 class="enc-title">Venutius</h1><p class="enc-meta"><span class="dates">fl. 50s–70s CE</span> · <a href="/centuries/1st-century">1st century</a></p></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">Venutius was a leader of the Brigantes of northern <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/britain">Britain</a> and consort of Queen <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/cartimandua">Cartimandua</a>. After their divorce he led the anti-Roman faction against her, and in 69 he drove her out of the kingdom, taking advantage of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/rome">Rome</a>'s civil war. His rebellion prompted the Roman conquest of the Brigantes under Petillius Cerialis from 71.</p><figure class="enc-fig"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/venutius.jpg" alt="Venutius" width="320" height="240" fetchpriority="high"><figcaption>Image: The original uploader was StephenDawson at English Wikipedia. · CC BY-SA 2.0 · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Great_Whernside.jpg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Wikimedia Commons</a></figcaption></figure></div>
<section class="enc-more"><h2 class="enc-more-h">Career</h2><p>Venutius first appears in the record in about 51 CE as the husband of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/cartimandua">Cartimandua</a>, queen of the Brigantes. Some scholars have suggested that he belonged to the Carvetii, a tribe that probably formed part of the Brigantian confederation. At that time the British resistance leader <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/caratacus">Caratacus</a>, defeated in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/wales">Wales</a> by <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/publius-ostorius-scapula">Publius Ostorius Scapula</a>, fled north to the Brigantes and was handed over to the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/roman-empire">Romans</a> by Cartimandua. The kingdom was nominally independent, but <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/tacitus">Tacitus</a> says that Cartimandua and Venutius were loyal to <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/rome">Rome</a> and &quot;defended by Roman power&quot;.</p><p>After the capture of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/caratacus">Caratacus</a>, Venutius became the most prominent leader of resistance to the Roman occupation. <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/cartimandua">Cartimandua</a> had married his armour-bearer, Vellocatus, and raised him to the kingship in Venutius's place. At first Venutius sought only to overthrow his former wife, and turned against her Roman protectors later. <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/rome">Rome</a> defended its client queen, and his revolt was defeated by Caesius Nasica while Aulus Didius Gallus was governor, between 52 and 57 CE.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">Legacy</h2><p>When Venutius rose again in 69, the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/roman-empire">Romans</a> could send <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/cartimandua">Cartimandua</a> only <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/auxilia">auxiliary troops</a>; she was evacuated and he took the kingdom. The revolt may have had wider effects. <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/tacitus">Tacitus</a> says that Vespasian, once emperor, had to recover <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/britain">Britain</a>, and that Britain had been abandoned when only just pacified, though some take this to refer to <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/gnaeus-julius-agricola">Agricola</a>'s later conquests in Caledonia. Nothing is recorded of Venutius after Vespasian's accession. <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/quintus-petillius-cerialis">Quintus Petillius Cerialis</a>, governor from 71 to 74, campaigned against the Brigantes, yet they were not fully subdued for many decades: Agricola appears to have fought in their territory, and both Juvenal and the Greek geographer <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/pausanias-geographer">Pausanias</a> refer to warfare against them in the first half of the second century.</p><p class="enc-more-src">This account follows the Wikipedia article “Venutius” (CC BY-SA 4.0), linked under Read further below.</p></section>
<section class="enc-quotes"><h2 class="enc-section-title">Across the Institute</h2><ul><li><blockquote>Cartimandua handed the fugitive Caratacus to the Romans in 51, and later her divorce from her consort <b>Venutius</b> split the Brigantes into rival factions.</blockquote><a href="/state/brigantes">Brigantes</a></li></ul></section>
